// Environmental writing for this reconstruction, inspired by the television
// silo. These are not episode transcripts or claims about canonical floors.
// Each numbered gallery has one distinct, physically mounted detail to find.
const records=`
The empty place|A cleaning-day seating slip has one name crossed out. Nobody has assigned the place again. Beyond the doorway, the wall screen still shows the hill.
A folded uniform|A laundry docket asks for a sheriff's uniform to be returned without pressing out the old repairs. Somebody has underlined the word returned.
Two signatures|A cleaning preparation sheet has two spaces for signatures. One is neat; the other is an ink blot where the pen stopped moving.
A lunch kept warm|The kitchen has saved a meal past the end of service. A note says to leave it for the porter coming up from the Mids.
The lottery envelope|An empty birth-lottery envelope is pinned beneath a family notice. The corners have been opened and flattened many times.
The erased name|A resident register has been rubbed almost through the paper. You can still see the pressure of a name that was meant to disappear.
The good pen|Someone has tethered the only working pen to the desk with three lengths of different string. The joining knots have been repaired too.
A second cup|A supplies tally orders two cups, then changes it to one. The second line was written with a trembling hand.
The quiet announcement|The loudspeaker repair card says it must work before the next public announcement. The technician has written: It already works. They cannot hear from here.
The borrowed shoes|A porter has left a drawing of a worn sole beside a request for new leather. The request has three rejection stamps.
One more flight|A child's staircase count is scratched into the lower edge of the service board. The last mark is much higher than the first.
The door seal|A flattened strip of sealant is pinned beside a pressure-door work order. A pencilled note asks who approved the replacement material.
A cleared shelf|A Judicial inventory lists an empty shelf as accounted for. The space where the confiscation receipt belonged is cleaner than the paper around it.
The red stamp|A relic classification card carries a red stamp so heavy it has split the paper. The name of the object was never filled in.
A careful correction|A Pact lesson has been corrected in two inks. Neither teacher has changed the sentence about asking to go outside.
The sealed parcel|A delivery receipt carries no sender's name. The destination was written, scraped away and written again: Janitorial.
The wrong cable|An electrician's sketch shows a thin cable continuing behind a mirror. The ordinary lighting circuit ends before it reaches the wall.
The number on the ring|An IT stock card records a numbered key ring without describing the lock. The space for an authorised borrower is blank.
The unbroken supply|A vault service ticket specifies uninterrupted power even during a generator shutdown. The rest of the floor has been assigned candles.
The clean gloves|A janitorial work order asks for clean gloves and no cleaning fluid. Someone has folded the instruction so only the destination shows.
A face turned away|A torn staff notice still carries its pinholes. Whatever picture was on it has been removed with more care than the text.
The second circuit|A technician has counted two electrical feeds where the public plan shows one. The correction has been circled but never signed.
The unlisted room|A corridor inspection sheet stops one door early. Its final tick sits beside a rectangle where no room number is printed.
The silent bell|A call-bell repair tag says the mechanism is sound. The wire has simply been disconnected at the far end.
The borrowed celebration|Festival bunting was issued, returned and issued again. The same small tear is marked on every inspection drawing.
A place at the bar|A drinks tally has a little duck sketched beside an unpaid line. The bar keeps the paper, although nobody is collecting the debt.
The mended ribbon|A ceremonial ribbon was shortened to remove a burn. The two surviving ends no longer meet unless somebody holds them together.
A song remembered|A scrap of music has words missing from every second line. Underneath, someone has written that their mother knew the rest.
The order of names|A memorial engraving order asks that the names stay in the family's order. An official has sorted them by resident number instead.
A cup without a handle|A household return slip refuses to discard a cracked cup. The reply offers a new handle, in a different colour.
The locked drawer|A furniture repair ticket asks for a new drawer bottom without opening the drawer. The carpenter has written a question mark.
The polished rail|A work note says not to paint over the bright patch on the handrail. People resting there have polished it with their palms.
A mother's measure|Three pencil heights and a date have been copied from a condemned doorframe. The carpenter saved the narrow strip of timber.
The spare blanket|A supplies request asks for an extra blanket for someone who is not on the household register. The request was quietly filled.
The school question|A child has asked why the word ocean appears in an old spelling list. The teacher has folded the sheet over that word.
The crooked frame|A picture-hanging work order measures the hooks exactly. The mirror was rehung crooked anyway; its owner says they prefer it that way.
A letter carried down|A porter asks for a letter to be delivered by hand. The instruction says not to put it in the outgoing tray.
The family recipe|A recipe uses a fruit nobody can identify. Every generation has crossed it out and substituted what the farms could provide.
The last page|A household book repair lists a missing final page. In the margin: Do not write an ending for us.
The new hinge|A salvaged hinge has outlasted three doors. The repair record gives it a longer history than any person using this gallery.
A candle stub|An outage kit has one candle less than its inventory. The note says it was used to read a letter, and will be replaced.
The old address|A parcel has followed a family across four levels. None of the porters has covered the earlier addresses.
A home in chalk|A child's drawing shows the familiar staircase, a bed and a kitchen. There are windows too, carefully rubbed out.
The borrowed hour|A wall-clock repair was delayed because two neighbours wanted one more evening before the correct time went back on the wall.
A stitch in blue|A school uniform repair uses blue thread on brown cloth. The family asked the seamstress to leave the repair visible.
The empty pram|A wheel has been repaired and oiled, although the pram is no longer in use. The repair ticket says to keep it ready.
The small shoes|Two sets of tiny shoe measurements share a sheet. The older set has a line through it; the newer set is larger.
An unapproved story|A lending card lists a story by its first sentence. The title was removed before the book was passed to the next household.
The garden promise|A seed envelope says the flowers are for someone who cannot make the stairs anymore. The sowing date is tomorrow.
Roots in the dark|A grower's sketch follows roots beneath the walkway. Somebody has drawn them reaching much farther than the bed allows.
The saved seed|A crop allocation has one extra seed held back, with a note saying it came from a plant worth remembering.
The careful watering|A watering schedule has been changed around an elderly resident's slow walk. The plants and the person now get the same daily visit.
The graft|A gardener has joined two damaged stems with a strip of cloth. The work card says both halves were nearly thrown away.
The light that stays|A plant lamp is marked for repair after the rest of the room goes dark. The grower asks for the seedlings to have one more night.
The taste of water|A filter worker has kept samples from before and after a repair. The label says the clear one is not always the clean one.
A dented jug|An allocation record notes the exact dent in a family's water jug. That dent has become its identifying mark.
The missing washer|A pump has been repaired with a washer cut from a discarded cooking pot. The maintenance drawing still has the pot's handle in it.
The patient valve|A valve takes three turns more than the handbook says. A handwritten arrow tells the next shift to be patient with it.
The leak caught early|A child's report of a wet wall is clipped above the engineering report. The engineer has credited the child by name.
The spare filter|A filter is signed out to a floor that did not request it. The porter has added: They needed it before they knew.
The lens request|A medical instrument requisition avoids the word magnification. Somebody has written it in pencil on the reverse.
The returned pages|A returns docket lists a children's travel book among ordinary medical texts. The clerk has put a question mark beside the word Georgia.
A repaired toy|A repair request describes a small mechanical toy with a stubborn spring. The instructions ask for it to be made to move once more.
The stitched cuff|A doctor's coat has been mended with workwear cloth from Down Deep. The laundry has carefully preserved the patch.
The patient letter|A note for a sleeping patient is folded around a drawing. There is no address, only a request that someone read it aloud.
The quiet visit|A visiting log has an extra chair pencilled beside the bed number. The visitor stayed longer than the register permitted.
A mother's tools|An instrument box is recorded as empty, but its lining still bears the shapes of small tools. The owner has kept the lining.
George's forwarding slip|A porter retained a forwarding address for G. Wilkins after the other records were cleared. The paper has been folded small enough to hide in a watch strap.
The remembered tune|A patient has asked the night staff to hum a tune they do not know. Someone has begun writing down its rhythm.
A room without flowers|A visitor brought a cutting rather than cut flowers. The request for a jar has been approved as medical equipment.
The good glass|A broken lens was set aside instead of ground down. The repair card asks whether seeing clearly should need permission.
The closed curtain|A curtain repair was done from the corridor so a patient could keep their privacy. The technician has refused to record what they heard.
A coat passed down|A family coat has six sets of initials inside its lining. Only the newest name is visible when the collar is folded normally.
The old lullaby|A parent has written a remembered lullaby on a ration envelope. Half of its places have no meaning to the children here.
The little machine|A child's mechanical toy has a replacement gear cut from brass scrap. Its maker asks for the broken gear back, to try again.
The saved button|A single button is listed as a complete repair kit. The note says it is the last one from a father's coat.
A borrowed book|A lending record has no due date. The owner has written: Bring it back when the story is finished with you.
The packed bag|A porter returned a travelling bag to its home floor. It had been packed for a journey farther than its owner could make.
The repaired handle|A walking stick has a new handle made from an old chair. The chair's resident number is still visible underneath.
A place to sit|A bench repair asks for one end to be left smooth. A regular visitor rests their bad hand there.
The letter never sent|An unsent letter was returned with a repaired desk. The carpenter did not read past the salutation.
The borrowed light|Two households are sharing a lamp while one circuit is repaired. Their children have started doing lessons together beneath it.
The worn threshold|A door repair record measures the hollow worn into the threshold. Generations have crossed it without anyone replacing the wood.
The hand-drawn sky|A blue scrap has tiny white points pricked through it. Held against a lamp, the holes make a pattern somebody thought worth keeping.
The longer route|A porter marks a detour to visit a resident who rarely gets visitors. The extra stairs are missing from the charge.
The parcel string|A parcel is tied with string knotted from several short pieces. The instructions ask for every piece to come back.
A message in chalk|A porter has left a chalk message for the next shift on the underside of the board. It is a warning about a loose step.
The weight on the slip|A delivery's declared weight was changed after a porter tried the first flight. The new figure includes the carrier's own judgement.
The repaired bell|A delivery bell has been fitted with a softer striker. The resident behind the door cannot bear a sudden noise.
The address by memory|A smudged address was delivered correctly. The porter wrote: I know whose handwriting this is.
A patient delivery|An urgent parcel waited while its porter helped someone on the stairs. Nobody has crossed out the urgent stamp.
The old route map|A route card shows an unused service passage. A newer stamp says closed; the pencil marks say somebody still remembers the way.
The spare strap|A carrying harness has a strap from a different maker. Its owner prefers the mismatch to having the whole thing replaced.
The last landing|A porter has marked a rest stop one flight before home. Even people who know every stair sometimes need to stop.
A note for Walker|A repair parcel is addressed only to Walker, Down Deep. No porter has needed a more precise address.
The disputed measure|A market weight was checked three times. Its owner has scratched a fourth mark on the back, where the inspector will not see it.
The stitched price|A price tag is sewn onto the cloth because its seller expects the same cloth to come back for another repair.
A familiar melody|A repaired radio briefly caught a tune between bands. The technician's report describes the sound instead of the frequency.
The saved tin|A market trader is selling an empty tin for more than the goods it once held. The shape is what the buyer remembers.
The repair space|A trader's counter list reserves one clear patch for a customer's watch. The cloth has been brushed smooth around its outline.
The scorched glove|A glove repair has been returned twice. The machinist keeps catching the same hot edge and will not surrender the glove.
The good bolt|A bin of mixed bolts has one wrapped separately in paper. The note says it fits the machine nobody can afford to stop.
A drawn tooth|A gear tooth is traced at full size beside a machinist's measurements. A correction says the trace, not the printed size, is right.
The borrowed bearing|A machine is running on a bearing borrowed from another department. Both departments have kept a copy of the promise to return it.
The hand on the wheel|A valve wheel has an uneven polish where one worker always grips it. The replacement order specifies keeping the old wheel.
A coat of dust|A cleaning note says not to brush the dust off a suspect joint. The dust is how the next shift will see whether it moved.
The marked spanner|A spanner has a notch cut into its handle so it can be identified by touch. The work light above its station fails often.
The warm pipe|An apprentice has labelled a warm pipe as a place to dry gloves. The supervisor has corrected the label, then used it too.
The short ladder|A ladder repair asks for the missing rung to be a different colour. Nobody wants the old break forgotten.
A hand-me-down torch|A torch is on its fifth battery clip. Every owner has scratched their initials beside the repair.
The saved offcut|A workshop has reserved a strip of brass too small to enter in stores. Somebody knows a repair that needs exactly that size.
The oil-stained note|A machine's best operating instructions are written on the back of a lunch wrapper. The clean manual stays shut.
The rejected roll|An adhesive sample failed the workshop's test even though the paperwork passed. The technician has kept both as evidence.
A better seal|A sealing strip is marked with the worker who tested it, not the department that issued it. The difference matters here.
The second attempt|Two broken prototypes are listed as material for a third. The requisition has been approved without a comment.
The humming wall|A night worker traced a vibration across three panels to its source. The diagram ends in a carefully drawn loose nut.
The apprentice's mark|An apprentice stamped their first repair crookedly. The supervisor has refused to let them grind it off.
A reading at zero|A gauge repair card includes a drawing of the needle at rest. The note says a good instrument must tell the truth when nothing happens.
The shared tool|A tool is signed out to an entire shift rather than one worker. Everyone's initials fit around the handle.
The repaired buckle|A harness buckle has been rebuilt from two broken ones. The stress test is recorded in a darker, firmer hand.
The little copper coil|A coil wound from recovered wire has a tag asking that it not be straightened for scrap. Somebody still wants to hear what it can do.
The listening bench|A bench repair asks for the loose panel to stay loose. A mechanic listens through it to the pipe behind the wall.
The heat mark|A scorched outline has been copied onto the replacement insulation. The crew wants to know if the heat comes back.
A thread of daylight|An old drawing calls a lamp sun. The word has been crossed out so often that the paper has worn through.
The mine lamp|A lamp returned from the workings has its glass cleaned from the inside. Its owner says that made more difference than a new bulb.
The counted boots|A shift sheet counts workers out and workers back. The return column is written more carefully than anything else on the page.
The red service band|A paint request calls for the same red on every section of a service pipe. Following the colour is easier than reading in the dust.
A name in the dust|A miner wrote a name on the service board between shifts. The cleaning crew has left a small rectangle untouched.
The rescued lunch tin|A dented lunch tin came back from a fallen workface. The repair order asks only that its lid should close again.
The cool handle|A handle has been wrapped with cloth where it becomes too hot to hold. The proper replacement is still on order.
The deeper sound|A maintenance note describes a sound beyond the working floor. The author has crossed out machine and written water.
The unused hook|A lifting-hook inspection gives its load rating, then adds that a person is not a load. The warning has been underlined twice.
The spare rope|A rope with one damaged section has been cut into two useful lengths. The knots are drawn on the back of the inventory card.
The old warning|A danger notice has been moved enough times to leave a brighter rectangle on the wall. The fasteners are newer than the words.
The crew's signature|An entire shift signed a repair that saved the generator from stopping. The last signature is small enough to fit between the others.
The held breath|A shutdown plan leaves one long blank interval between stopping the machine and starting it again. A pencil has written everybody waits here.
The next repair|A list of urgent repairs is longer than the sheet. A second piece of paper has been stitched to its bottom edge.
The protected scraps|A box marked scrap contains working parts wrapped in cloth. The stores clerk knows exactly why its label is wrong.
A letter from Supply|A note between workshops says the tested tape was sent, not the issued tape. The signature is hidden by the fold.
The workshop threshold|A delivery slip records a visit instead of a parcel. Someone from another department finally came all the way down the stairs.
The names beneath the paint|A maintenance board carries names scratched under an old coat of paint. The next coat was stopped just short of them.
The old cloth|A cleaning cloth has been washed and repaired until its original weave is hard to find. Its allocation slip calls it serviceable.
The sound below|A mechanic has recorded the sound heard when the generator pauses. Beneath the silence there is still water moving somewhere.
A way below|A last service note mentions a bulkhead, water and a light below the working floors. The drawing ends where the printed plan ends, but the pencil keeps going.
`.trim().split('\n').map((line,i)=>{const [title,text]=line.split('|');return Object.freeze({level:i+1,title,text,source:'Original environmental writing inspired by the TV series. This floor assignment is a game reconstruction.'});});
if(records.length!==144)throw new Error(`Expected 144 floor memories, found ${records.length}`);
export const FLOOR_MEMORIES=Object.freeze(records);
export const memoryFor=level=>FLOOR_MEMORIES[level-1]||null;
